That hunk was swept in from an uncommitted working tree while the #662 fix was
being committed. It is not a mistake in the code — it is sound and measured — but
it is not described by the message, so a bisect or blame landing here will
attribute the Empire's garrison behaviour to the Death Star Plans work.

What the garrison hunk does: the ground reserve previously covered subjugated
systems only, so the Empire would march every unit out of an Imperial-LOYAL
system. Reported twice — "AI Imperials are leaving Imperial loyal systems with no
units" (#625) and "moved ALL units away from an imperial loyal system... removing
the ability for Imperials to deploy or generate any units" (#632). It now also
holds one ground unit at loyal systems that PRODUCE, since a build skips any
system with opponent units present (rr p.3). The post-reveal exemption is
deliberately untouched: committing everything to the assault once the base is
exposed is the 2026-06-17 fix, and reserving at EVERY loyal system would re-strand
the force that diagnosis was about.

Measured, 30 self-play games, widening off vs on:

    producing Imperial systems held then emptied   140 -> 32   (4.67 -> 1.07/game)
    ...of those lost to Rebel loyalty by game end    8 ->  5

Strength unchanged: eval-strength over 1000 games gives Empire 272/500 both with
and without (harness verified deterministic across repeat runs). Expected — it
scores each side against a RANDOM opponent, and a random Rebel does not punish a
vacated producing system, so only the mechanism count can see the gain.

Evidence and instrumentation live in the follow-up commit bb3a5ee
(scripts/measure-imperial-vacate.mjs).

